From 1ecd259c67e04f8bd174b4e43efecdf1bc7c6cf6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:52:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] x11: list WM_DELETE_WINDOW on closable windows the gui
 decorates

With GSBackHandlesWindowDecorations (or GSX11HandlesWindowDecorations) NO,
-window:::: creates the X window without the window's style, keeping only
NSIconWindowMask and NSMiniWindowMask, and -_setSupportedWMProtocols: took
the closable bit from that stripped style.  No window the gui decorates
listed WM_DELETE_WINDOW, so a window manager asked to close one (Alt+F4, its
window menu, a task bar) had no way to ask the client to close it.  Mutter
then kills the client: closing a panel with Alt+F4 quits the whole
application at once, with nothing saved or asked.

-_setSupportedWMProtocols: now takes the closable bit from the NSWindow's
own style mask when the gui draws the decorations.  It runs again each
time a window is ordered in, when the NSWindow is known, so the protocols
are right before the window is mapped.  The window manager's request then
arrives as WM_DELETE_WINDOW and goes through -performClose:, as a click on
the gui's close button does.  Windows the window manager decorates are
unchanged.

Window managers only offer to close a window whose Motif hints allow it.
Today a window the gui decorates allows no functions, so Mutter doesn't
close it at all and Alt+F4 does nothing; the kill is reached once the
hints keep the window's functions (libs-back#244).  The two changes touch
different functions and apply in either order, but this one should not
come after #244.

Testing: Tests/x11/wmdeletewindow.m sets GSBackHandlesWindowDecorations NO
in the argument domain, orders in NSWindows of four styles and reads their
WM_PROTOCOLS back: a fully styled window and a closable, non-resizable one
must list WM_DELETE_WINDOW; a titled window that isn't closable and a
borderless one must not.  That covers the bug (closable windows missing
it) and guards against the fix spreading to windows that can't be closed.
It reads only the property the backend sets, so it needs a display but no
window manager.  Without this change the two closable checks fail (3 of 5
pass); with it all 5 pass.  The whole suite: 291 passed, none failed,
against 286 on master; the 5 more are the new checks.

Not covered by the test: what a window manager does with the protocol.
Under GNOME Shell 48.7 (X11, private Xvfb), with a small program showing a
closable window and panel, the gui drawing the decorations, Alt+F4:
- master, and master with this change: Mutter doesn't offer close
  (_NET_WM_ALLOWED_ACTIONS has no CLOSE), nothing happens;
- with #244 and without this change: the client is killed ("X connection
  broken");
- with #244 and this change: the panel, then the main window, close
  through -performClose: and the program keeps running.

The tests ran against libs-gui master (fdc5cfe83); libs-gui 0.32.0 aborts
the test process before its first window check (font roles, fixed in
8a092cfa7).

AI assistance: this change, its test and this description were written
with Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
